  #line-at(85.104pt, 95.9pt, "reconstructed spectra (color curves) of the CW laser, tuned from 1542.7 nm to 1559.6 nm, ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 123.5pt, "within the EO comb spectral range (1539–1561 nm at -10 dB, orange curve). Our results ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 151.1pt, "match the data (grey curves) measured by a commercial optical spectral analyzer (OSA, ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 178.7pt, "resolution of 20 pm), while achieving superior 10 MHz (0.08 pm) resolution with 100 µs ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 206.3pt, "acquisition time per spectrum. The signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) exceeds 50 dB, defined as ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 233.9pt, "the ratio of peak signal to noise floor deviation. Unlike conventional heterodyne detection ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 261.53pt, "between a comb and a test CW laser of unknown frequency, which requires multiple ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 289.13pt, "measurements to determine the absolute frequency, DCCS achieves this in a single ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 316.73pt, "measurement.  ")
  #line-at(96.984pt, 358.25pt, "Furthermore, DCCS has broadband reconstruction capability, enabling simultaneous ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 385.85pt, "measurements of multi-mode signals. Figure 2b illustrates measurements of a frequency-")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 413.45pt, "agile comb with ~130 evenly spaced modes (Methods). Compared to the OSA spectrum ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 441.07pt, "(20 pm resolution, red curve) with a 10 ms acquisition time, DCCS resolves the fine comb ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 468.67pt, "structure (0.16 pm resolution) in just 200 µs. Additionally, it provides phase information ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 496.27pt, "of the resolved modes (Fig. 2b, red line), setting it apart from conventional intensity-only ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 523.87pt, "spectrometers.  ")
  #line-at(96.984pt, 565.51pt, "Advantageously, DCCS can measure both coherent and weakly-coherent (or even ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 593.14pt, "incoherent) radiation. For example, we measured ASE spectra from a fiber laser in a non-")
