// arXiv source page 11
#original-page(594.96pt, 842.04pt, (image("graphics/page-11.png", width: 428pt), 83.5pt, 85pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(498.94pt, 790.2pt, "11 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(96.984pt, 95.9pt, "Next, we investigated transient behaviors in CW lasers, focusing on abrupt ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 123.5pt, "longitudinal-mode transitions (mode hopping) and multi-mode gain competition, as they ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 151.1pt, "critically affect laser stability and reliability. As a starting point, we measured an off-the-")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 178.7pt, "shelf semiconductor CW laser. When the pump current was switched on, the laser ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 206.3pt, "exhibited rapid, periodic mode hopping over a spectral span of 2 pm (≈250 MHz), as ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 233.9pt, "shown in Fig. 3a. Capturing such dynamics requires both high acquisition speed and fine ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 261.53pt, "spectral resolution—capabilities that remain challenging for conventional techniques. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 289.13pt, "Also, because standard TS-DFT lacks the ability to resolve continuous waveforms ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 316.73pt, "spectrally, it recorded only the temporal power (intensity) variations (blue curve in Fig. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 344.33pt, "3a). In contrast, DCCS achieved transient detection with a 10 μs integration time and 0.08 ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 371.93pt, "pm (10 MHz) resolution. Examples of the measured frequency modes and their phases ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 399.53pt, "are shown in Fig. 3b and Fig. 3c, respectively. Two-dimensional maps of phase evolution ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 427.15pt, "are provided in Supplementary Fig. S2. ")
  #line-at(96.984pt, 468.67pt, "We note that while LHR can be used for optical frequency measurement when the test ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 496.27pt, "laser frequency is approximately known, it becomes unsuitable for multi-mode lasers due ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 523.87pt, "to frequency ambiguity and limited detection bandwidth. DCCS overcomes this limitation. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 551.47pt, "To illustrate this, we built an FBG-based erbium-doped fiber ring laser emitting multiple ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 579.07pt, "wavelengths (see Methods). Using DCCS, we reconstructed the startup dynamics and ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 606.7pt, "revealed fast competition between two dominant modes (Fig. 3d) on a 10-μs time scale. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 634.3pt, "Figures 3e and 3f show zoom-in views of the frequency modes and phases, respectively. ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 661.9pt, "Although the spectra are shown within a narrow wavelength window (0.011 nm), our ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 689.5pt, "measurements actually covered a broader range defined by the dual combs. Transient, ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 717.1pt, "broadband measurements (spanning 1547–1553 nm) of a multimode semiconductor laser ")
  #line-at(85.104pt, 744.696pt, "are demonstrated in Supplementary Fig. S3. ")
]

